%S A308722 0,1,3,6,12,20,27,35,48,63,75,99,108
%N A308722 Number of edges in the smallest possible regular graceful graph of valence n.
%C A308722 A graceful graph with e edges is one that allows a labeling of its vertices with distinct integers between 0 and e, inclusive, such that when each edge is labeled with the absolute difference of the labels of its endpoints, these edge labels will be exactly the integers between 1 and e, inclusive.
%C A308722 This sequence counts the minimal number edges possible, in a regular graceful graph of valence n.
%C A308722 The concept was introduced by _Ed Pegg Jr_ in a post on Mathematics Stack Exchange; all the data here is from that post.
%C A308722 Pegg conjectured the continuation (from n = 13): 130, 147, 180, 192, 221, 243, 285. According to D. E. Knuth (personal communication, Oct. 5, 2026), Shisheng Li proved that no 16-regular graceful graph with 192 edges exists, ruling out the proposed value a(16) = 192. Knuth reports that the existence cases (e,k) = (200,16) and (243,18), where e is the number of edges and k the valence, remain open. - _Eric W. Weisstein_, Oct 06 2026
%C A308722 From _Shido Tamaki_, Oct 14 2026: (Start)
%C A308722 For the case (e,k) = (200,16) above, there exists a 16-regular graceful graph with 200 edges and 25 vertices. Its vertex labels are 0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 7, 8, 11, 22, 26, 42, 58, 74, 90, 106, 122, 138, 154, 170, 175, 182, 187, 197, 199, 200, and the edge list is provided in the linked file. By A004137 and Li's result mentioned above, no 16-regular graceful graph with at most 192 edges exists. Since the number of edges in a 16-regular graph must be a multiple of 8, it follows that a(16) = 200. The graph was found by applying integer linear programming to complete rulers of length 200 with 25 marks. It was checked by independent programs and formally verified in Lean 4 with Mathlib. The search programs and the Lean proof were written with the help of AI tools.
%C A308722 There exists a 14-regular graceful graph with 147 edges and 21 vertices. Its vertex labels are 0, 1, 2, 4, 6, 11, 17, 22, 36, 50, 64, 78, 92, 106, 120, 123, 132, 135, 143, 144, 147, one of the rulers listed by _Ed Pegg Jr_ in the Mathematics Stack Exchange question "Does a 16-valence 192-edge Graceful Graph exist?", and the edge list is provided in the linked file. A 14-regular graph with v vertices has 7v edges, and by A004137, no such graceful graph exists for v <= 20. Therefore, a(14) = 147. Similarly, A004137, together with Pegg's example in "Graceful graphs with Valence k", establishes that a(13) = 130. The graph was found by the same method and checked by independent programs. (End)
